/* Instructor-grounded simulated differential cases. Cases are authored simulations, not documented individual patients. */
window.VPQ_NECROPSY_DIFFERENTIAL_CASES={"version":4,"source":"Simulated pathology differential cases derived from instructor slide-audited Lectures 68, 69, 70, 72, 73, 74, 75, 77, 78 and 79; no actual patient records or slide images are reproduced.","cases":[{"id":"n68-brainstem","lecture":"68","system":"Neuro","title":"Circling ruminant: where is the lesion?","species":"Adult sheep","history":"An adult sheep fed poor-quality silage develops circling, head tilt, drooling and unilateral facial weakness. At necropsy, the brain has little obvious gross change; faint discoloration may be present in the brainstem.","gross":"Brainstem-focused lesion with little or no obvious gross change.","histo":"Multifocal neutrophilic microabscesses in brainstem parenchyma, sometimes mixed inflammatory cuffs around vessels.","best":"Listeriosis (Listeria monocytogenes)","morph":"Brainstem: multifocal, suppurative microabscessing encephalitis","mechanism":"Listeria can ascend via trigeminal and other cranial nerves by retrograde axonal transport, explaining the brainstem focus and asymmetric cranial nerve deficits.","tests":"Culture/PCR or other appropriate pathogen detection on relevant fresh tissues; necropsy localization and histology support but do not alone prove the agent.","ref":"Lecture 68 — Listeriosis, slide 10","options":[{"name":"Listeriosis (Listeria monocytogenes)","supports":"Silage exposure, circling, cranial nerve signs and brainstem localization all support this cause.","against":"Cannot confirm Listeria from these clinical signs alone.","hist":"Microabscessing suppurative rhombencephalitis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Thrombotic meningoencephalitis (Histophilus somni)","supports":"Ruminants may develop septic neurologic disease.","against":"Usually young feedlot cattle with random hemorrhagic foci and thrombosis/vasculitis rather than neural ascent to brainstem.","hist":"Septic vasculitis, thrombi and hemorrhagic infarction.","role":"less"},{"name":"Rabies (Lyssavirus)","supports":"Can cause cranial nerve signs and nonsuppurative brainstem inflammation.","against":"Typical rabies microscopy is nonsuppurative polioencephalomyelitis, not neutrophilic microabscesses.","hist":"Lymphocytic inflammation and possible Negri bodies (not invariably present).","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine protozoal myeloencephalitis","supports":"Asymmetric neurologic signs can occur with some CNS parasitic/protozoal conditions.","against":"Predominantly equine and frequently hemorrhagic/necrotizing spinal cord lesions; host argues strongly against.","hist":"Necrosis, hemorrhage, mixed inflammation and protozoal organisms occasionally detectable.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"n68-feedlot","lecture":"68","system":"Neuro","title":"Feedlot calf with hemorrhagic brain lesions","species":"8-month-old feedlot steer","history":"The steer develops fever and acute neurologic signs. Multiple irregular, dark-red foci are scattered at the cerebral gray–white interface.","gross":"Multifocal irregular cerebral hemorrhage and necrosis, sometimes with swelling or cloudy CSF.","histo":"Numerous small vessels show fibrinoid/necrotizing vasculitis, thrombi and adjacent infarction with hemorrhage.","best":"Thrombotic meningoencephalitis (Histophilus somni)","morph":"Brain: multifocal, hemorrhagic, necrotizing, thrombotic meningoencephalitis","mechanism":"H. somni bacteremia damages endothelium, initiating thrombosis, ischemic necrosis and leakage of blood. Distribution is vascular rather than perineural.","tests":"Bacterial testing of appropriate fresh CNS/systemic samples plus histopathology; consider systemic pneumonia, myocarditis or polyarthritis.","ref":"Lecture 68 — TME slides 11–12","options":[{"name":"Thrombotic meningoencephalitis (Histophilus somni)","supports":"Feedlot age, multifocal hemorrhage, gray–white junction lesions and thrombi/vasculitis are compelling.","against":"Agent still needs confirmatory identification.","hist":"Vasculitis, thrombosis, infarction.","role":"best"},{"name":"Listeriosis","supports":"Ruminant neurologic signs.","against":"Brainstem microabscesses and circling are more typical than multifocal cerebral vascular hemorrhage.","hist":"Neutrophilic brainstem microabscesses.","role":"less"},{"name":"Clostridium perfringens type D enterotoxemia","supports":"Can injure CNS vascular barrier and cause malacia.","against":"Often sheep/goats after ration change, preferentially bilaterally symmetric white-matter damage, rather than irregular thrombotic hemorrhages.","hist":"Edema and symmetric necrotic white matter foci.","role":"less"},{"name":"EHV-1 myeloencephalopathy","supports":"Vasculitis with thrombosis and hemorrhage can occur.","against":"Typically horses, not feedlot cattle; epidemiology argues strongly against it.","hist":"Small-vessel vasculitis, thrombosis and necrosis.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"n69-white-matter","lecture":"69","system":"Neuro","title":"Horse with acute cerebral malacia","species":"Adult horse","history":"A horse that has eaten contaminated moldy corn develops abrupt blindness, head pressing and seizures. On sectioning the brain, cerebral white matter is softened and liquefied while much of the gray matter is relatively spared.","gross":"Pale/yellow softened or cavitated regions concentrated in cerebral white matter.","histo":"Liquefactive necrosis and degeneration of white matter, with macrophages during lesion evolution.","best":"Fumonisin B1 equine leukoencephalomalacia","morph":"Cerebrum: multifocal to regionally extensive leukoencephalomalacia","mechanism":"Fusarium-associated fumonisin B1 toxicosis causes preferential severe injury to equine cerebral white matter. The professor notes its tissue selectivity is incompletely explained.","tests":"Feed/exposure history and toxin testing support the association; evaluate the lesion distribution and exclude other toxic/vascular causes.","ref":"Lecture 69 — Fumonisin B1 slide 35","options":[{"name":"Fumonisin B1 equine leukoencephalomalacia","supports":"Horse, moldy feed and white-matter liquefaction are the strongest combination.","against":"Specific fungal toxin exposure requires supporting feed/history evidence.","hist":"Severe white matter necrosis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Thiamine-deficiency polioencephalomalacia","supports":"Neurologic blindness, seizures and malacia.","against":"Classic ruminant lesion primarily affects cerebral cortical GRAY matter; species and location diverge.","hist":"Laminar cortical necrosis and softening.","role":"less"},{"name":"Centaurea-associated nigropallidal encephalomalacia","supports":"Horse toxic plant exposure and malacia possible.","against":"Preferential bilateral substantia nigra/globus pallidus lesions with chewing/prehension problems, not diffuse cerebral white matter injury.","hist":"Targeted neuronal necrosis in basal nuclei.","role":"less"},{"name":"Fibrocartilaginous embolism","supports":"Abrupt ischemic CNS malacia.","against":"Commonly spinal cord rather than this dietary-linked equine cerebral white matter syndrome.","hist":"Fibrocartilage occluding spinal vessel.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"n70-flaccid","lecture":"70","system":"PNS","title":"Acute flaccid paralysis with no gross nerve lesion","species":"Horse","history":"A horse develops progressively weak tongue, eyelids and pharyngeal muscles, then generalized flaccid paralysis after possible access to decaying carcass material. Gross sections of brain, cord and nerves appear unremarkable.","gross":"No characteristic gross CNS or PNS lesion despite severe neuromuscular dysfunction.","histo":"No specific microscopic lesion required; neurotoxin affects synaptic signaling rather than visibly destroying neurons.","best":"Botulism (Clostridium botulinum toxin)","morph":"Functional presynaptic neuromuscular blockade (often no characteristic morphologic lesion)","mechanism":"Botulinum neurotoxin blocks presynaptic acetylcholine release at neuromuscular junctions, causing flaccid paralysis.","tests":"Assess exposure history and use appropriate laboratory toxin testing in consultation with diagnostic laboratory; consider clinical differentials.","ref":"Lecture 70 — Botulism slides 18–19","options":[{"name":"Botulism (Clostridium botulinum toxin)","supports":"Flaccid generalized paralysis with cranial muscle involvement, carcass exposure and no structural lesion.","against":"Clinical presentation is not pathognomonic; toxin confirmation may be difficult.","hist":"No characteristic gross or microscopic lesion.","role":"best"},{"name":"Tetanus","supports":"Another clostridial neurologic toxicosis often without a gross lesion.","against":"Typical tetanus is SPASTIC rigidity due to loss of inhibitory motor control, not flaccid paralysis.","hist":"Often no characteristic neural lesion.","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine recurrent laryngeal neuropathy","supports":"Laryngeal dysfunction can contribute to abnormal airway signs.","against":"Chronic focal left laryngeal abductor muscle atrophy, not rapidly generalized paralysis of multiple muscle groups.","hist":"Distal axonal degeneration and denervation atrophy.","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine grass sickness","supports":"Autonomic/enteric dysfunction with minimal gross nerve lesions.","against":"Typically profound ileus, weight loss and dysautonomia rather than predominant neuromuscular junction paralysis.","hist":"Degeneration of autonomic ganglion neurons.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-crust","lecture":"72","system":"Skin","title":"Crusted muzzle lesions in a lamb","species":"Young lamb","history":"Several lambs develop proliferative crusts around their lips and muzzle after skin abrasions. A person handling them develops an ulcerated finger lesion. There is little respiratory illness in the affected lambs.","gross":"Thick raised proliferative crusting at mucocutaneous muzzle/lip sites.","histo":"Epidermal proliferation, ballooning degeneration and poxviral cytoplasmic inclusion bodies can occur.","best":"Contagious ecthyma (orf parapoxvirus)","morph":"Muzzle/lips: multifocal proliferative and crusting viral dermatitis","mechanism":"Parapoxvirus enters damaged skin and replicates in proliferating epidermal keratinocytes; zoonotic transmission is possible.","tests":"Clinical herd distribution and viral PCR where needed; use gloves and protective handling because lesions can be zoonotic.","ref":"Lecture 72 — Orf slide 11; sheeppox slides 6–7","options":[{"name":"Contagious ecthyma (orf parapoxvirus)","supports":"Young sheep, lip/muzzle proliferative crusts, abrasions and zoonotic human lesion.","against":"Poxvirus identity still requires testing if outbreak differential is important.","hist":"Proliferative viral epidermal injury and possible cytoplasmic inclusions.","role":"best"},{"name":"Sheeppox (capripoxvirus)","supports":"Sheep and skin papules/pustules/crusts can overlap.","against":"More generalized severe systemic disease, sparsely woolled skin and possible visceral lesions in many presentations.","hist":"Poxviral epidermal damage and inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophilosis (Dermatophilus congolensis)","supports":"Crusted exudative bacterial dermatitis can resemble pox crusts.","against":"Typical rain-associated fleece/dorsal disease and bacterial branching filaments; zoonotic transmission context and oral localization fit orf better.","hist":"Segmented Gram-positive branching 'railroad-track' organisms in crust.","role":"less"},{"name":"Fungal dermatophytosis","supports":"Scaling and crusted alopecic lesions may occur.","against":"Follicular circular alopecia and fungal hairs are more characteristic than proliferative mucocutaneous crusts.","hist":"Fungal hyphae/arthroconidia associated with hair/keratin.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-pruritus","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Itchy cats sharing a home","species":"Three domestic cats","history":"Two cats become intensely pruritic and overgroom, producing symmetrical ventral hair loss; a third cat has few signs. Surface tape samples may show small mites; deep follicular scrapings are less useful.","gross":"Self-induced symmetrical alopecia with variable subtle scaling and no obligate follicular pustules.","histo":"Superficial mites in stratum corneum if captured, rather than mites within hair follicles.","best":"Demodex gatoi infestation","morph":"Skin: superficial parasitic dermatitis / alopecia caused by grooming","mechanism":"D. gatoi is a contagious surface-dwelling feline Demodex species. Asymptomatic carriers can maintain transmission.","tests":"Superficial scrapings/tape and household contact assessment; confirm mite species and exclude allergic pruritus.","ref":"Lecture 73 — Feline Demodex cati versus gatoi slide 6","options":[{"name":"Demodex gatoi infestation","supports":"Multiple cats, contagious pruritus/overgrooming and surface-dwelling mites, with asymptomatic carrier.","against":"Mites may be difficult to find due to grooming; other pruritic dermatoses can coexist.","hist":"Mites in stratum corneum.","role":"best"},{"name":"Demodex cati infestation","supports":"Feline demodicosis may cause alopecia and sometimes pruritus.","against":"D. cati lives in follicles/sebaceous glands and is often associated with immunosuppression; does not fit surface mite clue.","hist":"Mites in hair follicles.","role":"less"},{"name":"Feline atopic dermatitis","supports":"Symmetric self-induced alopecia and pruritus fit.","against":"Contagion across cats and observed surface mites are not explained by atopy alone.","hist":"Variable hypersensitivity dermatitis without Demodex.","role":"less"},{"name":"Cheyletiellosis","supports":"Contagious superficial mites may affect multiple cats.","against":"Dorsal flaky 'walking dandruff' more characteristic than this ventral self-induced alopecia pattern.","hist":"Surface mites with scale.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-blister","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Pustules on muzzle and pawpads","species":"Middle-aged dog","history":"The dog develops symmetrical fragile pustules and thick crusts on the nasal planum, pinnae and pawpads. Several pustules have ruptured into collarettes and crusts.","gross":"Symmetrical superficial pustules and crusts at face and pawpads.","histo":"Subcorneal pustules with acantholytic keratinocytes and inflammatory cells; deep mucosal ulceration is absent.","best":"Pemphigus foliaceus","morph":"Skin: multifocal subcorneal acantholytic pustular dermatitis","mechanism":"Autoantibodies to keratinocyte desmosomal adhesion proteins, particularly desmocollin-1 emphasized in lecture, cause superficial loss of cohesion.","tests":"Biopsy a fresh intact pustule for histology; investigate infectious mimics such as staphylococcal pyoderma and dermatophytosis.","ref":"Lecture 74 — Pemphigus foliaceus slide 11, pemphigus vulgaris slide 12, bullous pemphigoid slides 14–15","options":[{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","supports":"Symmetric superficial pustules, face/pawpad distribution and subcorneal acantholysis fit.","against":"Acantholysis can also accompany some infections; correlate and exclude those.","hist":"Subcorneal acantholytic pustules.","role":"best"},{"name":"Pemphigus vulgaris","supports":"Immune-mediated acantholysis and erosions.","against":"Usually deeper suprabasal separation and severe mucosal/mucocutaneous ulcers.","hist":"Suprabasal acantholysis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bullous pemphigoid","supports":"Autoimmune blistering with erosions and crusting.","against":"Subepidermal cleavage at basement membrane, not subcorneal acantholytic pustules.","hist":"Subepidermal blister.","role":"less"},{"name":"Superficial bacterial pyoderma","supports":"Pustules, collarettes and crusts can look similar grossly.","against":"Histology plus cytology identifying cocci and bacterial follicular infection favor pyoderma; symmetric footpad distribution fits PF better.","hist":"Bacteria-associated neutrophilic folliculitis/pustules.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r75-testis","lecture":"75","system":"Repro","title":"Retained testis and feminization in a dog","species":"9-year-old intact male dog","history":"The dog has an abdominal retained testis, symmetrical alopecia and gynecomastia. At surgery, the retained testis contains a large firm white lobulated mass. CBC shows anemia and thrombocytopenia.","gross":"Large firm white fibrotic intratesticular mass in retained abdominal testis.","histo":"Neoplastic sustentacular Sertoli cells form cords/tubules, often within collagenous stroma.","best":"Sertoli cell tumor","morph":"Testis: Sertoli-cell neoplasm with suspected estrogen-associated marrow suppression","mechanism":"Some Sertoli neoplasms secrete estrogens and produce feminization; high estrogen can suppress hematopoiesis causing anemia, leukopenia or thrombocytopenia.","tests":"Testicular histopathology; evaluate cytopenias and clinical endocrine findings. Gross alone cannot prove hormone secretion.","ref":"Lecture 75 — Sertoli tumor slides 24/26 and cryptorchidism slides 13–14","options":[{"name":"Sertoli cell tumor","supports":"Abdominal cryptorchidism, firm white mass, feminization and cytopenias strongly support functional Sertoli tumor.","against":"Hormonal effects occur in only some cases and require clinical correlation.","hist":"Sertoli cell cords/tubules in firm fibrous stroma.","role":"best"},{"name":"Seminoma","supports":"Older dog and cryptorchid testis predisposition.","against":"Usually soft gray-white bulging tumor and nonfunctional; feminization and fibrotic cut surface favor Sertoli.","hist":"Sheets of neoplastic germ cells.","role":"less"},{"name":"Leydig cell tumor","supports":"Testicular tumor can sometimes have hormonal activity.","against":"Usually small yellow soft greasy nodule rather than large firm white fibrous mass.","hist":"Nests of interstitial endocrine cells.","role":"less"},{"name":"Suppurative orchitis","supports":"Testicular enlargement may arise from inflammation.","against":"Would expect inflammatory exudate and neutrophils rather than discrete white neoplastic mass with feminizing syndrome.","hist":"Neutrophilic testicular inflammation.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"r77-abortion","lecture":"77","system":"Repro","title":"Which placental lesion explains the abortion?","species":"Ewe with late-gestation abortion","history":"An ewe aborts in the last trimester. The fetus is well-preserved with few lesions. The cotyledons are dark red with dirty-red exudate; the intercotyledonary membranes are patchily thick, leathery and edematous. Another pregnant worker has helped handle abortion tissues.","gross":"Cotyledonary exudate AND extensive red-brown edematous/leathery intercotyledonary placentitis.","histo":"Necrotizing placentitis with inflammation/vasculitis can occur; intracellular organism identification requires ancillary tests.","best":"Chlamydia abortus (ovine enzootic abortion)","morph":"Placenta: multifocal to coalescing necrotizing exudative cotyledonary and intercotyledonary placentitis","mechanism":"C. abortus is an obligate intracellular zoonotic pathogen of sheep/goats; the instructor's speaker notes emphasize placental pathology in both compartments and late-pregnancy abortion.","tests":"Submit placenta and fetal samples for PCR/appropriate testing. Use zoonotic precautions, especially avoid exposure for pregnant people.","ref":"Lecture 77 Catch-Up PowerPoint speaker notes slides 40–41","options":[{"name":"Chlamydia abortus (ovine enzootic abortion)","supports":"Late ovine abortion, fetal preservation and BOTH placental compartments affected in characteristic leathery/exudative fashion.","against":"Other causes of severe placentitis overlap grossly; organism testing remains necessary.","hist":"Placental necrosis/vasculitis/inflammation; organisms can be difficult to distinguish without tests.","role":"best"},{"name":"Toxoplasma gondii","supports":"Ewe abortion and cotyledonary involvement.","against":"Classic distinctive scattered 1–3 mm white cotyledon nodules with largely normal intercotyledonary tissue, unlike broad leathery involvement.","hist":"Protozoal infection and focal placental necrosis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Coxiella burnetii (Q fever)","supports":"Late ovine abortion, thickened placenta, zoonotic risk and significant intercotyledonary exudate.","against":"Professor emphasizes abundant off-white exudate and often mineralization; can overlap heavily, so it remains a strong differential.","hist":"Marked placentitis with intracellular organisms; confirm by PCR.","role":"less"},{"name":"Campylobacter fetus subsp. fetus / C. jejuni","supports":"Sheep abortion and placentitis.","against":"More characteristic fetal targetoid liver necrosis when lesions are present; current description highlights placenta.","hist":"Fetal necrotizing hepatitis can occur.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"e78-corneal","lecture":"78","system":"Eye","title":"Dark corneal lesion in a cat","species":"Adult cat","history":"A cat has a history of recurrent corneal surface disease. A sharply defined brown-black plaque is present centrally on a cloudy cornea, with blood vessels extending toward the lesion.","gross":"Focal dark brown/black plaque surrounded by stromal opacity and corneal neovascularization.","histo":"Well-demarcated devitalized, acellular corneal stroma rather than intralesional hyphae or eosinophil-rich plaque.","best":"Feline corneal sequestrum","morph":"Cornea: focal stromal necrosis / sequestrum","mechanism":"Corneal sequestra represent focal stromal devitalization, often after chronic ulceration; precise initiating pathogenesis remains uncertain.","tests":"Ophthalmic exam and assessment for ulceration, depth and concurrent ocular disease; histopathology when tissue removed.","ref":"Lecture 78 — Corneal sequestrum slide 38; fungal and eosinophilic keratitis slides 34–35","options":[{"name":"Feline corneal sequestrum","supports":"Cat, focal dark devitalized plaque, surrounding opacity and neovascularization are characteristic.","against":"Concurrent corneal infection/ulceration may still require evaluation.","hist":"Acellular necrotic corneal stromal focus.","role":"best"},{"name":"Equine fungal keratitis","supports":"Corneal ulceration and severe necrosis can occur.","against":"More characteristic of horses; fungal hyphae and necrosuppurative keratitis versus focal black plaque in a cat.","hist":"Necrosuppurative keratitis with fungal hyphae.","role":"less"},{"name":"Feline eosinophilic keratitis","supports":"Cats may develop proliferative corneal plaques.","against":"Typically white-pink lateral corneal/conjunctival plaques, not a focal black necrotic sequestrum.","hist":"Predominantly eosinophilic infiltrates.","role":"less"},{"name":"Corneal dermoid","supports":"A congenital focal corneal lesion may resemble a mass.","against":"Dermoid contains hair follicles/adnexal glands rather than an acquired necrotic pigmented plaque.","hist":"Ectopic skin appendages in cornea.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"ear79-bulla","lecture":"79","system":"Ear","title":"Older Cocker spaniel with a keratin-filled bulla","species":"Older Cocker Spaniel","history":"The dog has a long history of recurrent otitis externa/media and new head tilt. Necropsy reveals an expansile cyst-like lesion in the tympanic bulla packed with pale flaky lamellated material.","gross":"Expansile middle-ear cyst with keratinous contents, rather than free liquid pus.","histo":"Keratin within an epidermal-lined lesion with fibrovascular stroma, granulomatous inflammation and cholesterol clefts/crystals.","best":"Tympanokeratoma (aural cholesteatoma)","morph":"Middle ear: keratinizing epithelial cyst / tympanokeratoma","mechanism":"Epidermal cyst associated with tympanic membrane/chronic otitis expands with retained keratin, compressing/eroding surrounding structures.","tests":"Histology of the middle-ear lesion, culture if infection coexists, and imaging as appropriate.","ref":"Lecture 79 — Tympanokeratoma slide 20, otitis media slide 18, inflammatory polyp slide 21","options":[{"name":"Tympanokeratoma (aural cholesteatoma)","supports":"Older Cocker Spaniel, chronic otitis and keratin-filled bulla with cholesterol granulomatous reaction.","against":"Concurrent bacterial otitis media is possible and should not be missed.","hist":"Keratin debris with fibrovascular/granulomatous stroma and cholesterol crystals.","role":"best"},{"name":"Suppurative otitis media","supports":"Chronic history, head tilt and middle-ear expansion.","against":"Would expect tan-yellow mucoid/purulent inflammatory exudate rather than an organized keratinous cyst.","hist":"Neutrophilic exudate ± bacteria.","role":"less"},{"name":"Aural inflammatory polyp","supports":"Middle-ear mass can protrude toward canal or nasopharynx.","against":"Most often young cats with smooth off-white pedunculated fibrovascular mass, not an older dog with packed keratin cyst.","hist":"Inflamed fibrovascular core lined by respiratory/squamous epithelium.","role":"unlikely"},{"name":"Ceruminous gland adenocarcinoma","supports":"Neoplastic ear masses may be locally invasive.","against":"Ceruminous gland tumors primarily external ear canal, cats overrepresented; not a typical lamellated keratin bulla cyst.","hist":"Invasive glandular neoplasia.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"n69-pem","lecture":"69","system":"Neuro","title":"Blind feedlot lamb: gray versus white matter","species":"6-month-old feedlot lamb","history":"After a rapid shift to a high-concentrate ration, a lamb becomes dull, develops cortical blindness and head-pressing, and then has seizures. No obvious ocular abnormality explains the blindness. The cerebral cortex is subtly swollen and yellow-gray; some gyri are flattened.","gross":"Bilaterally distributed cortical swelling, yellow discoloration and softening; chronic lesions may become cavitated. A Wood's lamp may demonstrate cortical autofluorescence, but absence does not exclude the disease.","histo":"Laminar neuronal necrosis in the cerebral gray matter, with neuronal eosinophilia and nuclear pyknosis, neuropil vacuolation, edema and variable macrophage reaction depending on duration.","best":"Polioencephalomalacia (cerebrocortical necrosis)","morph":"Cerebrum: bilateral, laminar, cortical neuronal necrosis (polioencephalomalacia)","mechanism":"Polioencephalomalacia describes the cortical lesion pattern, not one uniquely identified agent. In ruminants it can follow disruption of thiamine availability/metabolism or high sulfur exposure. Energy failure preferentially injures metabolically active cortical neurons.","tests":"Assess diet, ration transition, sulfur in water/feed and possible thiamine-related risk factors; evaluate response to treatment clinically. Histology establishes the lesion pattern but cannot distinguish sulfur-associated from thiamine-associated injury alone.","ref":"Lecture 69 — cortical necrosis/polioencephalomalacia topic; external veterinary diagnostic literature (confirm exact slide against lecture source)","options":[{"name":"Polioencephalomalacia (cerebrocortical necrosis)","supports":"Young ruminant, sudden blindness, ration change and bilaterally affected cerebral cortex strongly fit.","against":"Histology alone does not establish whether sulfur excess or thiamine disturbance was responsible.","hist":"Laminar cerebral cortical neuronal necrosis; gray-matter predominant.","role":"best"},{"name":"Fumonisin-associated equine leukoencephalomalacia","supports":"Seizures, blindness and cerebral malacia overlap.","against":"Equine disease with preferential cerebral white-matter damage; wrong species and tissue compartment.","hist":"Severe liquefactive WHITE-matter necrosis.","role":"unlikely"},{"name":"Lead toxicosis","supports":"Can cause acute cortical neurologic disease and blindness in ruminants.","against":"Requires a credible lead source and compatible systemic findings; cortical damage can overlap, so gross lesions alone are not decisive.","hist":"Cortical neuronal degeneration, edema and sometimes vascular lesions; lead confirmation relies on tissue/blood analysis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Salt poisoning / water deprivation","supports":"Can cause neurologic signs and cortical injury in some species.","against":"Strongly associated with pigs and relevant water/salt history; characteristic eosinophilic perivascular cuffing is more associated with porcine cases.","hist":"Laminar cortical neuronal injury, sometimes eosinophilic meningoencephalitis in pigs.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"n68-bse","lecture":"68","system":"Neuro","title":"Adult bovine: microscopic vacuoles and behavioral change","species":"5-year-old cow","history":"An adult cow develops a progressively abnormal temperament, hypersensitivity to sound and touch, incoordination and loss of condition. The brain appears grossly unremarkable; the teaching slide shows neuropil and neuronal vacuoles within selected brainstem nuclei.","gross":"No characteristic macroscopic CNS lesions despite progressive neurologic disease.","histo":"Bilateral symmetric spongiform vacuolation of neuropil, sometimes intraneuronal vacuoles, with neuronal loss and gliosis in characteristic brainstem regions. Distribution and validated testing are essential.","best":"Bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE)","morph":"Brainstem: spongiform encephalopathy (microscopic vacuolation)","mechanism":"Abnormally folded prion protein promotes misfolding and accumulation of host prion protein, leading to progressive neurodegeneration with little conventional inflammatory response.","tests":"Treat as a reportable prion-disease concern: contact the responsible regulatory veterinarian and diagnostic laboratory for approved collection and testing. Histologic vacuoles alone are not BSE-specific. Avoid unvalidated handling or testing claims.","ref":"Lecture 68 — spongiform encephalopathy/prion differential; USDA public-domain BSE histology reference (verify exact lecture slide alignment)","options":[{"name":"Bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE)","supports":"Adult bovine, progressive behavioral changes, ataxia and characteristic brainstem spongiform change support consideration.","against":"Cannot diagnose BSE on history or vacuolation alone; laboratory confirmation and exclusion of other causes are necessary.","hist":"Characteristic spongiform change involving selected brainstem nuclei with little inflammation.","role":"best"},{"name":"Rabies","supports":"Behavior change, hypersensitivity and neurologic dysfunction require consideration.","against":"Rabies generally features nonsuppurative encephalitis rather than the characteristic spongiform distribution.","hist":"Lymphocytic polioencephalitis, possible intracytoplasmic Negri bodies; confirmation uses validated assays.","role":"less"},{"name":"Toxic/metabolic vacuolar encephalopathy","supports":"Some toxicoses and metabolic conditions can cause neuropil/myelin vacuolation.","against":"Distribution, clinical course and ancillary evidence determine cause; vacuoles are not prion-specific.","hist":"Variable white matter or neuropil vacuolation and lesion distribution.","role":"less"},{"name":"Listeriosis","supports":"Common ruminant CNS infection can produce ataxia and cranial nerve deficits.","against":"Usually focal/asymmetric brainstem signs with suppurative microabscesses rather than noninflammatory spongiform change.","hist":"Suppurative microabscessing rhombencephalitis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r76-granulosa","lecture":"76","system":"Female repro","title":"Mare with behavior change and one enlarged ovary","species":"11-year-old mare","history":"A mare has intermittent stallion-like behavior and failure to exhibit regular estrus. Rectal palpation and ultrasound identify one markedly enlarged multicystic ovary and a small, relatively inactive contralateral ovary. The ovarian cut surface is pale yellow-white with numerous cystic spaces.","gross":"Unilateral enlarged, smooth, solid-to-multicystic ovarian mass, yellow-white cut surface; contralateral ovarian atrophy.","histo":"Neoplastic granulosa cells in follicle-like microstructures (Call-Exner bodies may be present), cords or sheets, often with theca-cell proliferation; histologic architecture varies.","best":"Granulosa-theca cell tumor","morph":"Ovary: unilateral sex cord–stromal neoplasm (granulosa-theca cell tumor)","mechanism":"Functional neoplastic sex cord–stromal cells may produce inhibin and androgenic or estrogenic steroids, inhibiting follicle development in the opposite ovary and altering estrous or stallion-like behavior. Hormone patterns vary.","tests":"Ultrasonography, anti-Müllerian hormone and inhibin/testosterone where indicated; histopathology of removed ovary establishes subtype. Hormone concentrations are supportive, not substitutes for tissue diagnosis.","ref":"Lecture 76 catch-up PowerPoint, slides 3–4 speaker notes (ovarian tumor and granulosa-theca cell tumor)","options":[{"name":"Granulosa-theca cell tumor","supports":"Unilateral multicystic mass, contralateral atrophy and hormone-associated behavioral change fit.","against":"Some tumors are solid and not hormonally active; ultrasound alone cannot classify every ovarian mass.","hist":"Sex cord–stromal cords/follicular patterns; possible Call-Exner bodies.","role":"best"},{"name":"Persistent anovulatory follicle / follicular cyst","supports":"Ovarian cystic enlargement and abnormal cycling may overlap.","against":"Less convincing for a large mixed solid multicystic tumor with marked contralateral suppression and prolonged behavioral changes.","hist":"Cystic follicle lined by granulosa cells, without neoplastic sex cord proliferation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Ovarian epithelial cystadenoma","supports":"Ovarian epithelial tumors may form large multicystic masses.","against":"Typically lack the functional endocrine and contralateral atrophy pattern.","hist":"Cysts lined by neoplastic epithelial cells, sometimes papillary.","role":"less"},{"name":"Ovarian teratoma","supports":"Germ-cell neoplasms may enlarge an ovary.","against":"Expected heterogeneous tissue such as hair, bone or cartilage; endocrine effects less characteristic.","hist":"Differentiated tissues from multiple germ layers.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"r76-pyometra","lecture":"76","system":"Female repro","title":"Intact dog with distended uterine horns","species":"9-year-old intact female dog","history":"Several weeks after estrus, a dog develops lethargy, polyuria/polydipsia and anorexia without visible vaginal discharge. Both uterine horns are markedly enlarged and fluid-filled. The endometrium bears numerous small cystic glands.","gross":"Tensely distended uterine horns containing thick yellow-green suppurative material; cystic endometrial glandular change. An apparently closed cervix can prevent vaginal discharge.","histo":"Neutrophilic luminal exudate, endometrial glandular cystic hyperplasia, variable mucosal necrosis and inflammatory extension depending on severity.","best":"Canine pyometra associated with cystic endometrial hyperplasia","morph":"Uterus: diffuse suppurative endometritis with luminal pus (pyometra), associated with cystic endometrial hyperplasia","mechanism":"Progesterone-dominant diestrus increases glandular secretion and reduces uterine contractility/clearance; ascending opportunistic bacteria, frequently E. coli, can establish infection and trigger endotoxemia.","tests":"Urgent systemic evaluation, imaging, CBC/chemistry and bacterial culture where appropriate; histopathology to assess inflammation and CEH. Clinical suspicion warrants timely stabilization and treatment.","ref":"Lecture 76 slides 17 and 21–24: CEH, metritis, canine pyometra and species distinctions","options":[{"name":"Canine pyometra associated with cystic endometrial hyperplasia","supports":"Diestrus, PU/PD, systemic illness, luminal pus, and neutrophilic endometritis.","against":"Gross purulence alone cannot identify bacterial species or degree of systemic compromise.","hist":"Suppurative endometritis with or without CEH.","role":"best"},{"name":"Mucometra","supports":"Distended uterus containing viscous material can mimic a fluid-filled pyometra on imaging.","against":"Sterile mucus without marked neutrophilic exudate or illness does not explain the suppurative findings.","hist":"Mucus-filled lumen with little/no suppurative inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Hydrometra","supports":"Fluid-distended uterus could be similar grossly.","against":"Thin watery nonpurulent fluid and minimal uterine inflammation would favor hydrometra.","hist":"Watery luminal fluid without a major neutrophilic exudate.","role":"less"},{"name":"Postpartum metritis","supports":"Fetid uterine exudate and systemic bacterial illness are possible.","against":"Recent parturition or retained fetal membranes would usually be expected; metritis affects multiple uterine-wall layers.","hist":"Often more diffuse full-thickness uterine-wall inflammation.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-campy","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Aborted lamb with distinctive liver lesions","species":"Late-gestation ewe and aborted fetus","history":"Several ewes abort late in gestation. One fetus has multiple approximately round pale target-like foci in the liver; the placenta is thickened and exudative. Other aborted fetuses have no obvious characteristic hepatic lesions.","gross":"Fetal liver: multifocal targetoid pale necrotic foci, sometimes with red or raised margins. Placenta may be edematous, necrotic and covered in exudate.","histo":"Suppurative to necrotizing placentitis, placental bacterial microcolonies, sometimes vasculitis, fetal pneumonia and random necrosuppurative hepatitis.","best":"Ovine Campylobacter-associated abortion (C. jejuni or C. fetus subsp. fetus)","morph":"Placenta: necrosuppurative placentitis; fetus: multifocal necrotizing hepatitis, consistent with bacterial abortion","mechanism":"Maternal intestinal exposure and bacteremia permit placental colonization and fetal infection; a compromised exchange surface and direct fetal infection lead to abortion. Target-like hepatic lesions are strongly suggestive but not pathognomonic.","tests":"Submit fresh placenta, fetal abomasal contents, lung and liver for appropriate culture/PCR (laboratory guidance); fixed placenta and fetal organs for histology. Observe zoonotic abortion-tissue precautions.","ref":"Lecture 77 PowerPoint slides 36–38 speaker notes (Campylobacter species and target lesions)","options":[{"name":"Ovine Campylobacter-associated abortion (C. jejuni or C. fetus subsp. fetus)","supports":"Late abortion with multifocal targetoid fetal liver necrosis and necrosuppurative placentitis is a high-yield pattern.","against":"Characteristic liver foci are inconsistent among cases and are not sufficient to identify the species.","hist":"Bacterial placentitis with microcolonies; fetal necrosuppurative hepatitis and pneumonia.","role":"best"},{"name":"Chlamydia abortus","supports":"Late ewe abortion with severe placentitis and little fetal pathology is common.","against":"Typically striking intercotyledonary leathery change and dirty red-brown exudate rather than targetoid fetal liver lesions.","hist":"Necrotizing placentitis, sometimes vasculitis; organism requires specific testing.","role":"less"},{"name":"Coxiella burnetii","supports":"Ruminant abortion with severe placentitis, possibly with few fetal lesions.","against":"Fetal targetoid hepatic foci are less characteristic, and many cases have limited fetal changes.","hist":"Severe trophoblast-rich necrotizing placentitis; intracellular bacteria may be abundant.","role":"less"},{"name":"Brucella melitensis","supports":"Zoonotic ovine/caprine late abortion with placentitis and fetal pneumonia.","against":"No unique liver target signature; laboratory confirmation and public health precautions are necessary.","hist":"Necrotizing placentitis and fetal bronchopneumonia.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-ehv","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Sudden third-trimester abortion in a mare","species":"Pregnant mare, approximately month 10","history":"A mare with no obvious preceding fever or reproductive discharge aborts a relatively fresh fetus. The fetal lungs do not fully collapse, are heavy and edematous; the liver has multiple small pale foci, and the thymus may show petechiae. The placenta has no dominant cervical-star exudative lesion.","gross":"Fresh fetus with pulmonary edema and small pale foci in liver and/or lungs, possible body-cavity fluid and petechiae; gross findings may be mild or absent.","histo":"Multifocal hepatic necrosis with intranuclear viral inclusions in hepatocytes and sometimes other affected cells; pulmonary and lymphoid tissue injury can occur.","best":"Equine herpesvirus 1 abortion","morph":"Fetus: multifocal hepatic necrosis and pulmonary edema, consistent with viral fetal infection","mechanism":"Maternal EHV-1 infection can produce cell-associated viremia and fetal/placental infection late in gestation; fetal systemic infection and tissue necrosis follow, often without warning to the dam.","tests":"PCR on appropriate fetal lung, liver, thymus and placenta; histopathology and diagnostic laboratory guidance. Apply farm biosecurity during abortion investigation.","ref":"Lecture 76 slide 34 (EHV-1 abortion); Lecture 77 fetal abortion investigation slides 26–29","options":[{"name":"Equine herpesvirus 1 abortion","supports":"Abrupt late abortion, fresh fetus, pulmonary edema and multifocal hepatic necrosis strongly support.","against":"Some EHV-1 abortions lack distinct lesions; PCR is needed to confirm.","hist":"Hepatic necrosis with possible eosinophilic intranuclear inclusions.","role":"best"},{"name":"Ascending bacterial placentitis","supports":"Common cause of equine abortion and fetal infection.","against":"Usually suppurative placentitis prominent at cervical star; fetal liver viral-type inclusions would not fit.","hist":"Neutrophilic chorioallantoitis with bacteria; fetal bronchopneumonia possible.","role":"less"},{"name":"Leptospiral abortion","supports":"Can cause late equine abortion with placental edema and fetal liver lesions.","against":"Often requires renal/placental pathogen evidence; typical viral intranuclear inclusions not explained.","hist":"Variable nephritis, hepatitis and placentitis without viral inclusions.","role":"less"},{"name":"Umbilical cord torsion","supports":"May cause sudden noninfectious fetal hypoxia and death.","against":"Cord twisting/vascular compromise should be apparent; does not explain viral inclusion-bearing hepatic necrosis.","hist":"Hypoxic/vascular tissue injury, no infectious cytopathic inclusions.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-mummy","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Small retained fetal remains: mummy or maceration?","species":"Multiparous sow","history":"A sow farrows several healthy piglets with two much smaller dark, desiccated, intact fetal forms. These have leathery skin and are not foul-smelling. No purulent uterine fluid is evident. Determine the process before assuming an infectious cause.","gross":"Shrunken dry brown-black fetal remnants maintaining fetal outline, without gas, pus or marked decomposition.","histo":"Extensive postmortem dehydration and autolysis; little diagnostic intact fetal histology is expected. Absence of purulent uterine inflammation supports an aseptic retention process.","best":"Fetal mummification","morph":"Uterine contents: retained desiccated fetuses (mummification)","mechanism":"Fetal death after sufficient skin development and retention in a relatively sterile uterine environment permits water resorption while a continuing pregnancy or luteal function prevents immediate expulsion. The morphology is an outcome, not an etiologic diagnosis.","tests":"Assess gestational stage, sizes and count of affected fetuses, placenta, sow reproductive history and herd pattern. In swine consider porcine parvovirus among causes of variable-size mummies; determine etiology separately by appropriate fetal/placental testing.","ref":"Lecture 77 catch-up PowerPoint slides 16–20 speaker notes (mummification, maceration, emphysematous fetus); Lecture 76 slide 29 PPV/SMEDI","options":[{"name":"Fetal mummification","supports":"Sterile-appearing, dark leathery, intact dry fetus in mixed-size litter.","against":"Cannot establish which infection or noninfectious event caused fetal death.","hist":"Desiccated autolyzed retained fetal tissues.","role":"best"},{"name":"Fetal maceration","supports":"Retained dead fetal tissue could also break down over time.","against":"Maceration generally involves bacterial infection, fetal tissue disintegration and pus, often with retained bones.","hist":"Suppurative uterine inflammation and decomposed fetal remains.","role":"less"},{"name":"Emphysematous fetus","supports":"Dead retained fetus may become infected.","against":"Would expect gas-producing putrefaction, swelling, crepitation and malodor, not dry intact tissues.","hist":"Putrefactive tissue changes with bacterial overgrowth.","role":"unlikely"},{"name":"Normal stillbirth","supports":"Perinatal fetal death can accompany a live-born litter.","against":"Normal-size fresh stillborn fetus does not explain chronically retained, small dry fetal remnants.","hist":"Variable findings depending on death timing; not characteristic dry mummification.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-toxo","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"White cotyledonary foci and fetal brain necrosis","species":"Ewe with late-pregnancy loss","history":"An ewe aborts in late gestation. Several cotyledons have small well-demarcated pale spots; intercotyledonary membranes are not uniformly leathery. Histology has yet to be interpreted. A regional veterinarian is investigating the flock's fetal losses.","gross":"Multiple pale pinpoint-to-millimeter necrotic/mineralized foci centered on placental cotyledons; fetal gross lesions may be absent.","histo":"Multifocal necrotizing placentitis with protozoal organisms in trophoblasts; fetal nonsuppurative necrotizing encephalitis can be present. Demonstration of parasite antigen/DNA strengthens etiologic attribution.","best":"Toxoplasma gondii-associated ovine abortion","morph":"Placenta: multifocal necrotizing cotyledonary placentitis; fetus: multifocal necrotizing encephalitis","mechanism":"Maternal ingestion of sporulated feline-origin oocysts permits parasitemia and transplacental infection; infected trophoblast and fetal tissues develop necrosis. Fetal outcome depends in part on gestation stage.","tests":"Placental and fetal brain PCR plus histology and immunohistochemistry where available; maternal serology alone does not prove abortion etiology. Protect pregnant/immunocompromised handlers from fetal and placental tissues.","ref":"Lecture 77 abortion and placentitis differential; Dorsch et al. 2022, Front Vet Sci Figure 3 (open-access CC BY diagnostic case)","options":[{"name":"Toxoplasma gondii-associated ovine abortion","supports":"Cotyledonary white foci and fetal necrotizing encephalitis fit; placental histology can demonstrate protozoa.","against":"Cotyledonary pallor alone is not diagnostic; need specific tissue confirmation.","hist":"Focal cotyledonary trophoblast necrosis with protozoal organisms; fetal necrotizing encephalitis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Campylobacter-associated ovine abortion","supports":"Late abortion and necrotizing placentitis are compatible.","against":"More characteristic fetal targetoid liver foci and bacterial placental microcolonies; do not confuse these with white cotyledon foci.","hist":"Necrosuppurative placentitis with bacterial microcolonies; fetal hepatitis/pneumonia.","role":"less"},{"name":"Chlamydia abortus","supports":"Late ewe abortion with placentitis.","against":"More typically extensive dirty red-brown leathery intercotyledonary chorioallantoitis.","hist":"Necrotizing placentitis, with specific agent demonstrated by PCR/IHC.","role":"less"},{"name":"Coxiella burnetii","supports":"Marked ruminant placentitis with little fetal injury can occur.","against":"Cotyledonary pale foci plus fetal necrotizing encephalitis are not its strongest pattern.","hist":"Severe necrotizing placentitis, often numerous intracellular bacteria in trophoblasts.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r75-ram-epididymis","lecture":"75","system":"Male repro","title":"Ram with a firm unilateral epididymal tail","species":"3-year-old breeding ram","history":"At breeding soundness examination the ram has decreased fertility and a hard enlarged cauda epididymidis on one side. A cut section shows fibrous thickening, focal creamy debris and distended ducts containing inspissated sperm. Some affected rams in the flock have no palpable changes.","gross":"Firm enlarged epididymal tail, fibrosis and multifocal abscess-like or sperm-granulomatous foci; testis may be atrophic.","histo":"Chronic interstitial lymphoplasmacytic and suppurative epididymitis, duct obstruction, sperm leakage and foreign-body granulomatous reaction; bacterial identification requires ancillary testing.","best":"Brucella ovis-associated epididymitis","morph":"Epididymis: chronic active, fibrosing, sperm-granulomatous epididymitis","mechanism":"B. ovis establishes chronic reproductive-tract infection; duct inflammation and obstruction rupture sperm-containing ducts, exposing sperm antigens to the interstitium and generating granulomatous inflammation, fibrosis and impaired sperm transport.","tests":"Brucella ovis flock testing/serology and organism-specific PCR or culture under diagnostic laboratory guidance. Palpation alone misses subclinical carriers; local reporting and herd policies vary.","ref":"Lecture 75 slides 19–20 (ram epididymitis: Brucella ovis versus Actinobacillus seminis)","options":[{"name":"Brucella ovis-associated epididymitis","supports":"Sexually mature ram, characteristic cauda epididymis involvement, flock infertility and possible subclinical carriers.","against":"Clinical and gross appearance alone cannot distinguish all bacterial causes.","hist":"Chronic active epididymitis with periductal fibrosis and sperm granulomas.","role":"best"},{"name":"Actinobacillus seminis epididymitis","supports":"Can cause pyogranulomatous epididymitis and infertility in rams.","against":"More often affects younger rams; significant overlap means culture/PCR is decisive.","hist":"Suppurative/pyogranulomatous epididymitis, not etiologically distinct by H&E alone.","role":"less"},{"name":"Noninfectious sperm granuloma after trauma","supports":"Ruptured ducts release sperm and generate granulomatous reaction.","against":"Flock-associated epididymitis and infectious inflammation increase suspicion of infection.","hist":"Foreign-body giant-cell inflammation around extravasated sperm, with little bacterial evidence.","role":"less"},{"name":"Testicular seminoma","supports":"May cause a firm gonadal mass and infertility.","against":"Primary lesion is epididymal rather than a discrete intratesticular germ-cell tumor.","hist":"Sheets of neoplastic germ cells within testis.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"r75-prostate","lecture":"75","system":"Male repro","title":"Older dog with pelvic outlet obstruction and bone pain","species":"11-year-old neutered male dog","history":"A dog has tenesmus, dysuria, progressive weight loss, and reluctance to walk. Rectal examination reveals an irregular firm fixed prostate; imaging shows mineralization, sublumbar lymphadenopathy and new proliferative lesions of lumbar vertebrae.","gross":"Irregular infiltrative mineralized prostate, sometimes asymmetrical, with firm adjacent tissue and regional lymph node enlargement.","histo":"Infiltrative malignant epithelial cells in irregular tubules, nests or solid sheets, marked atypia and stromal desmoplasia. Tumor type and origin may require immunohistochemistry.","best":"Canine prostatic carcinoma","morph":"Prostate: infiltrative malignant epithelial neoplasm (prostatic carcinoma)","mechanism":"Malignant prostatic epithelium or urothelial-type carcinoma infiltrates local structures, obstructs urinary/fecal passage and may metastasize to nodes, lungs and axial skeleton.","tests":"Imaging and tissue/cytology assessment with histopathology and immunohistochemistry as needed; distinguish primary prostatic from urothelial carcinoma. Metastasis staging is clinically important.","ref":"Lecture 75 slides 28–31 (prostatic cysts, prostatitis, benign hyperplasia and carcinoma)","options":[{"name":"Canine prostatic carcinoma","supports":"Firm irregular mineralized prostate, neutered status and possible axial skeletal metastases.","against":"Prostatic mineralization and inflammation can overlap; obtain tissue characterization.","hist":"Infiltrating atypical glandular/urothelial-like epithelial cells.","role":"best"},{"name":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia","supports":"Older male dog with prostatomegaly and straining.","against":"Usually intact, smoother and symmetric enlargement; infiltrative fixed lesion plus metastases does not fit.","hist":"Orderly hyperplastic glands with epithelial proliferation and minimal invasion.","role":"less"},{"name":"Prostatic abscess / bacterial prostatitis","supports":"Can cause painful enlargement, focal cavities and dysuria.","against":"Fever, infection evidence and purulent exudate would support infection; osseous lesions are not typical metastases from abscess.","hist":"Suppurative inflammation and destruction, sometimes bacterial colonies.","role":"less"},{"name":"Paraprostatic cyst","supports":"May cause marked pelvic mass effect and straining.","against":"Usually an extraprostatic cystic structure, rather than fixed mineralized infiltrative gland.","hist":"Cyst wall with variable lining, not infiltrative malignant glands.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"r76-mastitis","lecture":"76","system":"Mammary","title":"Sudden agalactia versus gangrenous mastitis","species":"Postpartum dairy cow","history":"A cow has sudden severe depression, fever, and greatly reduced milk production in one quarter. The secretion is thin, watery and blood tinged. The affected gland is markedly swollen and edematous, with sharply demarcated dusky necrotic areas and systemic signs of endotoxemia.","gross":"Marked edema and hemorrhage, necrotic mammary tissue and watery abnormal milk from one involved quarter.","histo":"Severe neutrophilic and necrotizing mastitis, with vascular injury and edema; histology alone does not reliably identify an individual coliform species.","best":"Acute severe coliform (E. coli) mastitis","morph":"Mammary gland: acute severe necrosuppurative and hemorrhagic mastitis","mechanism":"Ascending intramammary Gram-negative bacterial infection activates powerful innate inflammatory responses, including LPS-driven systemic inflammatory and vascular effects that damage the gland and cause shock.","tests":"Aseptically collected milk culture/PCR as appropriate, assessment for systemic inflammatory response and shock, fluid/electrolyte status, and clinical management. Necrosis pattern is not organism-specific.","ref":"Lecture 76 slides 37–42 (coliform, staphylococcal, streptococcal, and Mycoplasma mastitis)","options":[{"name":"Acute severe coliform (E. coli) mastitis","supports":"Peracute severe systemic illness, one affected quarter, watery/bloody secretion and necrotizing edema.","against":"Staphylococcal gangrene can look very similar; culture needed.","hist":"Acute necrotizing neutrophilic mastitis and marked edema/vascular injury.","role":"best"},{"name":"Staphylococcus aureus gangrenous mastitis","supports":"Can cause gangrenous blue-black mammary necrosis and severe toxemia.","against":"Clinical pattern and histology overlap; culture rather than gross color proves agent.","hist":"Necrosuppurative mastitis and often coccal aggregates.","role":"less"},{"name":"Mycoplasma bovis mastitis","supports":"Agalactia and rapidly affected herd mates are typical.","against":"Often multiple quarters, firm swollen udder and floccular milk with less overt severe endotoxemic crisis.","hist":"Variable suppurative mastitis; confirmation with specific molecular testing/culture.","role":"less"},{"name":"Streptococcal mastitis","supports":"Milk quality changes and inflamed quarter are plausible.","against":"Often less striking vascular necrosis and shock in comparison; overlap possible.","hist":"Neutrophilic mastitis with bacteria, requiring organism testing.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-mycotic","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Leathery bovine placenta with fetal skin plaques","species":"Dairy cow, last third of pregnancy","history":"A cow aborts a late-gestation fetus. The placenta has thick, opaque, leathery intercotyledonary membranes and enlarged necrotic cotyledons. Raised gray-white crusting lesions are present on the fetal head and neck. A section of placenta reveals abundant branching hyphae invading chorionic tissue.","gross":"Necrotizing and thickened leathery placenta, sometimes adventitial placentation, with focal gray-white raised fetal skin plaques and possible pneumonia.","histo":"Necrotizing placentitis with invading fungal hyphae; septate acute-angle branching hyphae favor Aspergillus, whereas broader pauciseptate hyphae favor certain Mucorales. Morphology should be confirmed by culture or molecular methods.","best":"Mycotic abortion, most suggestive of Aspergillus spp.","morph":"Placenta: severe necrotizing fungal placentitis with fetal mycotic dermatitis","mechanism":"Environmental fungi enter maternal circulation and colonize placentomes, spread through fetal membranes and impair placental exchange; placental and fetal infection cause fetal death. Exact fungal species cannot be established by gross morphology alone.","tests":"Submit fresh and fixed placenta, fetal skin lesions, lung and stomach contents; perform fungal culture/PCR, H&E, PAS and GMS stains. Species identification requires laboratory support.","ref":"Lecture 77 slide 44 speaker notes (Aspergillus-associated bovine abortion); veterinary diagnostic pathology literature","options":[{"name":"Mycotic abortion, most suggestive of Aspergillus spp.","supports":"Leathery intercotyledonary placentitis plus fetal fungal dermatitis and invading septate hyphae.","against":"A mold species must not be named from gross appearance alone; Mucorales differ microscopically.","hist":"Fungal invasion of placental stroma/vessels; septate hyphae on special stain.","role":"best"},{"name":"Brucella abortus abortion","supports":"Necrotizing placentitis and fetal pneumonia occur.","against":"Fungal hyphae in placenta and fetal skin favor mycotic etiology; brucellosis requires specific testing.","hist":"Necrosuppurative placentitis with bacteria rather than true tissue-invasive fungal hyphae.","role":"less"},{"name":"Neospora caninum abortion","supports":"Important bovine transplacental cause of late abortion.","against":"Usually fetal nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis, not dense fungal placentitis and cutaneous plaques.","hist":"Necrotizing nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis with protozoal organisms.","role":"less"},{"name":"Chlamydial placentitis","supports":"Necrotizing placentitis can occur in ruminants.","against":"Does not produce tissue-invasive branching hyphae.","hist":"Trophoblast necrosis/inflammation with intracellular bacteria, not fungi.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"r77-neospora","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Bovine fetus with encephalitis but few gross lesions","species":"Cow aborting at 6 months gestation","history":"A beef herd experiences several mid-gestation abortions and repeat losses among certain dam lines. The fetuses are often autolyzed with no dramatic external lesions. Fetal brain and heart histology shows scattered areas of necrosis and mononuclear inflammation.","gross":"Often no distinctive gross fetal lesion; autolysis can obscure findings. Occasionally focal pale tissue changes.","histo":"Multifocal necrotizing nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis, often with glial response; intralesional protozoal cysts/tachyzoites may be sparse.","best":"Neospora caninum-associated bovine abortion","morph":"Fetus: multifocal necrotizing nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis","mechanism":"Transplacental Neospora infection, including endogenous reactivation in persistently infected dams, injures fetal neural and cardiac tissues; vertical transmission explains recurrence in dam lines.","tests":"Fetal brain/heart histopathology with Neospora PCR and immunohistochemistry where available; dam serology informs herd risk but does not alone establish causation.","ref":"Lecture 77 slide 46 speaker notes (Neospora caninum bovine abortion)","options":[{"name":"Neospora caninum-associated bovine abortion","supports":"Mid-gestation cattle abortions, recurrence in dam lines, fetal nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis.","against":"Protozoal morphology may be sparse and Toxoplasma/other etiologies can overlap; confirm by specific tests.","hist":"Multifocal necrotizing nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Bovine viral diarrhea virus fetal infection","supports":"Can cause fetal loss and neurologic malformations.","against":"Hydranencephaly, cerebellar hypoplasia or ocular lesions depend on infection timing; necrotizing myocarditis plus protozoal encephalitis is more suggestive of Neospora.","hist":"Developmental CNS abnormalities or nonspecific inflammatory/necrotic changes.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bovine herpesvirus 1 abortion","supports":"Can produce fetal hepatic necrosis and abortion.","against":"Hepatic multifocal necrosis, sometimes viral intranuclear inclusions, more typical than neural/cardiac protozoal pattern.","hist":"Fetal hepatocellular necrosis with possible intranuclear inclusions.","role":"less"},{"name":"Toxoplasma gondii","supports":"Related protozoan can cause fetal necrotizing encephalitis.","against":"Ovine/caprine abortion more characteristic; bovine abortion due to Toxoplasma is uncommon, species testing essential.","hist":"Protozoal-associated necrotizing encephalitis; immunostains/PCR differentiate.","role":"less"}]}]};
